 # Procedural and implicit volumes
 
-Also called: Analytic Volumes, Procedural Geometry, Implicit Fields
 
 ## Overview
-Procedural and implicit volumes generate volumetric data on-the-fly using mathematical functions rather than storing discrete voxel arrays or acquiring physical scans. This family solves the problem of infinite resolution and compact storage by evaluating scalar fields $f(\mathbf{x})$ at query points, enabling seamless zooming, dynamic deformation, and complex boolean compositions without explicit mesh or grid storage. It differs from [Voxelization](12-voxelization.md) and [Acquisition](14-acquisition.md) by being analytic rather than sampled, and from [Simulation output](15-simulation-output.md) by being deterministic and stateless rather than derived from physical time-stepping.
+Procedural and implicit volumes generate volumetric data on-the-fly using mathematical functions rather than storing discrete voxel arrays or acquiring physical scans [G]. This family solves the problem of infinite resolution and compact storage by evaluating scalar fields $f(\mathbf{x})$ at query points, enabling seamless zooming, dynamic deformation, and complex boolean compositions without explicit mesh or grid storage [G]. It differs from [Voxelization](12-voxelization.md) and [Acquisition](14-acquisition.md) by being analytic rather than sampled, and from [Simulation output](15-simulation-output.md) by being deterministic and stateless rather than derived from physical time-stepping [G].
 
 ## Shared principles
-All methods in this family map a conceptual or physical source into a discrete or analytic volumetric representation suitable for rendering. The core mechanism involves:
-1.  **Domain Definition**: Establishing a spatial domain, typically a 3D grid with resolution $(N_x, N_y, N_z)$ and bounding box, or an infinite analytic space [S1, S3, unofficial].
-2.  **Value Assignment**: Determining the value (density, distance, color) for each point in the domain based on the source method (geometric intersection, function evaluation, or physical state) [S1, S12].
-3.  **Storage/Access**: Either storing the result in a discrete array (voxelization, simulation) or providing an interface to compute it on-the-fly (procedural) [S1, S3, unofficial].
+See [Producing volume data](11-producing-volume-data.md) for shared principles.
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Chunk Generation with Base Terrain](1110-chunk-generation-with-base-terrain.md)
-A method for automatically populating voxel data when a new chunk is created or accessed in a procedural world. It initializes empty spatial containers by defining a deterministic "base" geometry (such as a flat platform) that serves as the foundational layer for further procedural modifications. It focuses on discrete block placement logic rather than continuous scalar field evaluation.
+Populates voxel data when a new spatial chunk is created or accessed, establishing a deterministic initial state (e.g., a flat platform) before higher-level features are applied. It sets itself apart by focusing on structural chunk initialization rather than surface noise variation, and from [Cloud Mesh Generation](4822-cloud-mesh-generation.md) by generating solid terrain blocks rather than volumetric fluid meshes. Choose it when initializing procedural worlds with discrete block-based terrain.
 
 ### [Procedural Volume Rendering with Noise Perturbation](1370-procedural-volume-rendering-with-noise-perturbation.md)
-Enhances coarse, low-resolution volumetric data by superimposing high-frequency details using a small, tiling procedural noise volume. It solves the problem of "blobby" or overly smooth appearances in real-time rendering by creating wispy, cloud-like filaments or fur-like textures without increasing the underlying voxel resolution. It perturbs sampling coordinates or optical properties at render time, effectively moving the view through a "solid space" texture.
-
-### [Distance to Hypertextures](1555-distance-to-hypertextures.md)
-A technique for adding geometric relief and procedural detail to surfaces without increasing polygon count, by evaluating procedural texture synthesis within a volumetric region around the object. It deforms implicit surfaces by adding procedural noise to the function's range (value) instead of the domain (coordinates), which enables the use of sphere tracing with safe distance bounds.
+Enhances coarse volumetric data by superimposing high-frequency details using procedural noise, creating wispy, cloud-like filaments suitable for real-time clouds, smoke, and fire. It sets itself apart by avoiding the computational cost of increasing voxel resolution or recomputing geometric gradients; instead, it warps the sampling domain or modulates optical properties at render time. It differs from [Procedural Noise Functions](1799-procedural-noise-functions.md) by specifically targeting volume access coordinates or transfer function outputs rather than generating the base field itself. Choose it when adding high-frequency detail to low-resolution volumes without re-sampling.
 
 ### [Procedural Noise Functions](1799-procedural-noise-functions.md)
-Generates continuous or discrete scalar fields from spatial coordinates without storing explicit geometry, solving the problem of creating organic, non-repetitive, or structured volumetric detail (such as clouds, smoke, terrain, and cellular structures) in real-time rendering. Unlike static texture lookups, these methods evaluate mathematical functions on-the-fly, allowing for infinite resolution and seamless tiling.
+Generates continuous or discrete scalar fields from spatial coordinates to produce organic, structured, or cellular volumetric detail without storing explicit geometry. This family is distinct from [Gradient-based Noise (Perlin/Simplex)](3860-gradient-based-noise-perlin-simplex.md) and [Value Noise](4008-value-noise.md) by encompassing distance-based cellular patterns, flow-advection techniques, and specific application-driven post-processing. Choose it when generating base volumetric fields or textures on-the-fly.
 
 ### [Implicit Surface Primitives](1800-implicit-surface-primitives.md)
-Defines 3D geometry as the zero-level set (isosurface) of a scalar field function $f(\mathbf{x})$, where the surface is the locus of points where $f(\mathbf{x}) = 0$. This family solves the problem of representing complex, smooth, or dynamic geometry without polygonal meshes, enabling operations like boolean blending, smooth deformation, and efficient ray marching.
-
-### [Implicit Field Composition](2652-implicit-field-composition.md)
-Constructs a complex volumetric or surface field by combining multiple primitive scalar fields (such as terrain, spheres, and cuboids) using Boolean set operations. It solves the problem of defining intricate scene geometry analytically without explicit mesh storage, serving as the primary input for marching cubes or dual contouring algorithms.
+Represents 3D geometry as the zero-level set of a scalar field function $f(\mathbf{x})$, solving the problem of representing complex, smooth, or dynamic geometry without polygonal meshes. It is distinguished from polygonal representations by its continuity and from procedural noise volumes by its focus on sharp or smoothly blended boundaries rather than volumetric density gradients. Choose it for boolean blending, smooth deformation, and efficient ray marching of implicit shapes.
 
 ### [Weather & Cloud Parameterization](3617-weather-cloud-parameterization.md)
-Defines the physical and visual configuration for volumetric cloud rendering, controlling coverage, density, type, geometry, lighting, and noise interaction. It solves the problem of generating coherent, physically plausible cloud structures by mapping high-level weather data to low-level volumetric properties that drive procedural noise generation and raymarching integration.
+Defines the global physical and visual configuration for volumetric cloud rendering, controlling parameters such as coverage, density, cloud type, geometry bounds, lighting angles, and noise interaction weights. It differs from its siblings by providing the input data structures and global constants rather than the algorithmic implementation of density field generation or light integration. Choose it to decouple high-level atmospheric state from low-level procedural noise generation and raymarching integration.
 
 ### [Cloud Mesh Generation](4822-cloud-mesh-generation.md)
-[Not given in the sources.]
+Constructs a discrete 3D mesh representation of volumetric cloud data by identifying contiguous regions of non-zero density within a procedural voxel grid. It solves the problem of rendering large-scale, procedurally generated cloud fields efficiently by converting sparse voxel data into a compact triangle or quad mesh, rather than ray-marching the volume directly. It differs from [Procedural Volume Rendering with Noise Perturbation](1370-procedural-volume-rendering-with-noise-perturbation.md) by outputting geometry (mesh) instead of a shader-based volume texture, and from [Chunk Generation with Base Terrain](1110-chunk-generation-with-base-terrain.md) by focusing on horizontal planar structures rather than vertical terrain topology. Choose it when converting procedural voxel clouds into renderable mesh geometry.
 
 ### [Signed Distance Function (SDF) Generation and Processing](5342-signed-distance-function-sdf-generation-and-processing.md)
-Covers methods for generating, processing, and utilizing Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) as volumetric data for rendering and geometry representation. It solves the problem of representing continuous 3D surfaces and thin structures without explicit mesh extraction by treating the zero-level set of the SDF as a volumetric boundary. Unlike standard volume rendering which takes density directly, this approach derives density from the distance to the implicit surface.
+Covers methods for generating, processing, and utilizing Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) as volumetric data for rendering and geometry representation. Unlike standard volume rendering which takes density directly, this approach derives density from the distance to the implicit surface, enabling the representation of open surfaces and varying thickness through an exponential falloff. Choose it when representing continuous 3D surfaces without explicit mesh extraction or requiring resolution-independent thickness.
+
+### [Cartoon Cloud Density Generation](8309-cartoon-cloud-density-generation.md)
